          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Gehenna is the Valley of Hinnom transformed into a symbol of final divine judgment and punishment of the wicked. Traditional Christianity usually construes that punishment as eternal conscious suffering, while annihilation, historical judgment, and purgative readings are recognized alternatives.\",\"book_summary\":\"Gehenna is the judgment-symbol rooted in the Valley of Hinnom’s idolatry and prophetic designation as the Valley of Slaughter; it culminates in a destructive verdict against both body and soul and is distinguished from Sheol.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Gehenna symbolizes final divine judgment upon the wicked.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Gehenna symbolizes a final judgment whose verdict destroys both body and soul.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book selects the annihilationist or destructive interpretation of Gehenna and thereby excludes eternal conscious torment, temporary purgation, and a merely historical judgment as adequate descriptions of its outcome. It retains the consensus category of final divine judgment but specifies its result more narrowly.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"The book’s destructive verdict conflicts with the dominant traditional view if “destroys both body and soul” means cessation of existence, since that cannot simultaneously be everlasting conscious punishment. It does not contradict the minimum cross-response consensus that Gehenna signifies final judgment, and its interpretation is explicitly represented among the listed alternatives.\",\"rationale\":\"After reducing both readings to their minimum cores, they share the same central referent: the Valley of Hinnom as a symbol of divine judgment. The book does not replace that referent; it resolves the disputed nature of the judgment in favor of destruction. This is therefore a material narrowing rather than a match, divergence, or novel identification.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 10:28 supports destruction of body and soul, and Isaiah 66:24 depicts corpses, but these texts do not conclusively establish annihilation; Mark 9’s unquenchable fire and undying worm have historically supported eternal punishment. The printed citations strongly establish the valley background and judgment symbolism, but only partially establish the book’s specific destructive outcome and do not directly develop its distinction from Sheol.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
